## Why This Is Asked
This question probes ability to design multi-protocol contract governance, versioning, and cross-protocol mappings in a real product. It tests practical approaches to keep REST, GraphQL, and gRPC synchronized, handle migrations with minimal disruption, and implement tests and observability.

## Key Concepts
- Single source of truth for OpenAPI, SDL, and proto contracts
- Versioned envelopes and per-endpoint migrations
- Deprecation windows and non-breaking changes
- Cross-protocol mappings (REST -> GraphQL -> gRPC)
- Consumer-driven contract tests, validation, and observability (tracing/metrics)

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal versioned contract envelope
class ContractEnvelope {
  constructor(version, restOpenAPI, graphqlSDL, grpcProto) {
    this.version = version;
    this.rest = restOpenAPI;
    this.graphql = graphqlSDL;
    this.grpc = grpcProto;
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you automate contract generation and validation across all three protocols?
- How would you handle breaking changes and feature flag migrations without affecting existing clients?
- What testing strategy would you use to ensure cross-protocol semantics stay in sync over time?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests per-tenant Saga resilience, isolation, and recovery with Event Sourcing and outbox.

## Key Concepts

- CQRS and Event Sourcing
- Saga orchestration and compensations
- Per-tenant isolation and outbox pattern
- Idempotent event handlers and replay

## Code Example

```javascript
// Minimal orchestrator sketch (pseudo)
function startSaga(tenantId, jobId){
  dispatchEvent({tenantId, jobId, type:'IngestRequested'});
}
function onEvent(event){
  if(event.type==='IngestSucceeded') emit('TranscodeRequested', event);
  if(event.type==='IngestFailed' || event.type==='TranscodeFailed' || event.type==='WatermarkFailed'){
    emit('Compensate', {tenantId:event.tenantId, jobId:event.jobId, steps:['Ingest','Transcode','Watermark']});
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you ensure exactly-once processing for the tenant outbox?
- How would you test failure injection and crash recovery in this Saga?

## Why This Question Is Asked

Tests the ability to design scalable, highly available job queues with tenant isolation and live rebalancing.

## Key Concepts

- Per-tenant sharding and hot-tenant handling
- Replica synchronization and consistency models (CP vs AP)
- Online shard rebalancing with lease-based ownership
- Delivery semantics (at-least-once vs exactly-once) and deduplication
- Failure modes, observability, and testing under churn

## Code Example

```javascript
// Acquire shard lease for a tenant
const shardHandle = await acquireShardLease(tenantId);

// Enqueue job to tenant's shard
async function enqueueJob(tenantId, job) {
  const shardId = computeShard(tenantId, job.id);
  const shard = await getShardOwner(shardId);
  return shard.enqueue({ tenantId, job, timestamp: Date.now() });
}

// Idempotent job processing
async function processJob(job, shard) {
  const acquired = await shard.acquireLock(job.id, leaseDuration);
  if (!acquired) return { status: 'skipped' };

  const processed = await checkProcessed(job.id);
  if (processed) return { status: 'duplicate', jobId: job.id };

  await executeJob(job);
  await markProcessed(job.id);
  await shard.releaseLock(job.id);

  return { status: 'completed', jobId: job.id };
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
